CAT = "accafaContext"; SEG = "CTX"
Q = [
("Which type of business entity is a separate legal person from its owners?",
 "A limited liability company", ["A sole trader", "A partnership", "A trader's registered business name"],
 "A company is incorporated, so it can own assets and enter contracts in its own name, and its shareholders' liability is limited. A sole trader and a partnership have no legal identity separate from their owners."),
("Compared with trading as a sole trader, a disadvantage of trading as a limited liability company is:",
 "More regulation and public disclosure", ["Unlimited liability for business debts", "Difficulty in raising share capital", "No separate legal identity"],
 "Companies must file and publish financial statements and follow company law, which costs time and money. Unlimited liability and no separate identity are features of a sole trader, not a company."),
("Which body develops and issues IFRS Accounting Standards?",
 "The IASB", ["The IFRS Foundation trustees", "The IFRS Interpretations Committee", "The IFRS Advisory Council"],
 "The International Accounting Standards Board sets IFRS Accounting Standards. The IFRS Foundation oversees and funds the standard-setting boards, and the Advisory Council advises them."),
("The main role of the IFRS Interpretations Committee is to:",
 "Give guidance where standards are unclear", ["Fund and oversee the IASB's work", "Advise the IASB on its work plan", "Set sustainability disclosure standards"],
 "The Interpretations Committee issues interpretations on issues not specifically covered or open to differing treatment. Sustainability disclosure standards are set by the ISSB, and the Advisory Council gives the boards strategic advice."),
("Who is responsible for preparing a company's financial statements?",
 "The directors", ["The external auditors", "The shareholders", "The tax authorities"],
 "Directors are responsible for preparing financial statements that give a true and fair view and for keeping adequate accounting records. Auditors report on the statements; they do not prepare them."),
("Which user group is mainly interested in whether a business will pay amounts it owes within the agreed credit period?",
 "Suppliers", ["Employees", "Government agencies", "Equity investors"],
 "Suppliers who give credit want to know whether invoices will be paid when due. Employees focus on job security and pay, and investors on returns and the value of their shares."),
("Under the Conceptual Framework, an asset is a present economic resource that the entity:",
 "Controls as a result of past events", ["Owns legally at the reporting date", "Expects to buy in the next year", "Has paid for in full in cash"],
 "The definition rests on control, not legal ownership or payment. An item bought on credit is still an asset, and an item to be bought next year is not yet one."),
("Equity is the residual interest in an entity's assets after deducting:",
 "All of its liabilities", ["Its current liabilities only", "Dividends paid in the year", "Its non-current assets"],
 "Equity equals assets less all liabilities, current and non-current. This is the accounting equation rearranged."),
("Financial statements are normally prepared on the basis that the entity will continue to trade for the foreseeable future. This is the:",
 "Going concern basis", ["Accrual basis", "Consistency concept", "Prudence concept"],
 "Going concern justifies carrying assets at cost less depreciation rather than at break-up values. If the entity were to close, the statements would be prepared on a different basis."),
("A sole trader takes goods from the business's inventory for her family's use and records them as drawings. Which concept is she applying?",
 "The business entity concept", ["The materiality concept", "The duality concept", "The offsetting principle"],
 "The business is treated as separate from its owner, so goods the owner takes are drawings, not business expenses. Duality is the two-sided effect of every transaction."),
("A business sells goods to a bank and must buy them back next year at the same price plus interest. Treating this as a loan rather than a sale applies:",
 "Substance over form", ["Prudence", "Historical cost", "Going concern"],
 "The legal form is a sale, but the business keeps the risks and rewards of the goods and pays interest, so in substance it has borrowed money secured on its inventory."),
("Information is material if omitting or misstating it could reasonably be expected to:",
 "Influence users' decisions", ["Exceed 5% of profit before tax", "Change the auditor's opinion", "Breach a loan covenant"],
 "Materiality is judged by its effect on the decisions of primary users, considering size and nature. A percentage of profit is only a rule of thumb, not the definition."),
("Under the Conceptual Framework, the two fundamental qualitative characteristics of useful financial information are:",
 "Relevance and faithful representation", ["Comparability and verifiability", "Timeliness and understandability", "Prudence and consistency"],
 "Comparability, verifiability, timeliness and understandability are enhancing characteristics. Information must first be relevant and faithfully represented to be useful."),
("A business uses the same inventory valuation method every year so that users can compare results over time. This mainly supports:",
 "Comparability", ["Timeliness", "Verifiability", "Relevance"],
 "Consistent policies let users identify similarities and differences between periods and entities, which is comparability. Timeliness is about having information in time to influence decisions."),
("Financial information that is complete, neutral and free from error has which qualitative characteristic?",
 "Faithful representation", ["Relevance", "Understandability", "Timeliness"],
 "Completeness, neutrality and freedom from error are the three parts of faithful representation. Relevance is about capacity to make a difference to decisions."),
("A business nets a bank overdraft at one bank against a cash balance at another, though it has no legal right of set-off. Which principle does this break?",
 "The offsetting principle", ["The materiality concept", "Accrual accounting", "The business entity concept"],
 "Assets and liabilities, and income and expenses, are not offset unless a standard requires or permits it. Without a right of set-off, the cash and the overdraft are shown separately."),
]
